<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="what-a-plugin-can-do" title="What a plugin can do">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>A <code>WeaverPlugin</code> extends the engine, and only a weaver your own program
        builds loads one. The build plugin is in <a href="maven-goals.topic">Maven goals</a>,
        the editor plugin in <a href="tooling.topic">Tooling</a>.</p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        You implement one interface, <code>WeaverPlugin</code>, and register everything you add
        from a single <code>contribute</code> call. Six methods on <code>PluginContext</code>
        take a registration, and what you register arrives through them. Loading the plugin
        itself reaches further. You compile against <code>aether-weaver-api</code> and read
        views, never an engine type. Even one that registers nothing moves the plan's fingerprint and
        the class's <code>@Woven</code> stamp. Both record its namespace and version.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    P["Your WeaverPlugin"] --> C["One contribute call"]
    C --> A["Injection points: identifiers an @At may name"]
    C --> B["Injector kinds, and instructions behind each"]
    C --> M["Metadata written into every woven class"]
    C --> O["An opt-in for classes that were rewritten"]
    A --> E["The weaver, while it plans and rewrites"]
    B --> E
    M --> E
    E --> V["Four events your plugin observes"]
    O --> V
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="What reaches the weaver" id="surface">
        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="Injection points">
                <p>
                    <code>points(...)</code> registers the identifiers an <code>@At</code> may
                    name, and builds the point behind each one. A point decides <b>where</b>, and
                    answers with the positions it found. Every identifier you offer is written
                    <code>namespace:NAME</code>, and
                    <a href="namespaces.topic">Namespaces and identifiers</a> has the rule.
                    <a href="injection-points.topic">Injection points</a> covers what the built-in
                    ones already find.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Injector kinds">
                <p>
                    <code>injectors(...)</code> registers the kinds a hand-built
                    <code>InjectorSpec</code> may carry. No annotation lets a weave name a
                    contributed kind: <code>@Inject</code>, <code>@Redirect</code> and
                    <code>@Wrap</code> are the only ones an annotated weave can carry.
                    <a href="contribute-an-injector.topic">Contribute an injector</a> covers building one by hand. A
                    kind no injector was registered for is <code>AW4090</code> and costs that one
                    declaration; install the plugin that registers it on the weaver being built,
                    with <code>plugin(...)</code> or <code>discoverPlugins(...)</code>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Metadata">
                <p>
                    <code>metadata(key, value)</code> records entries under your namespace; the
                    count and length limits are in <a href="spi.topic#throws">The plugin SPI</a>.
                    Exceeding one throws, and a throw out of <code>contribute</code> costs the
                    plugin its entire contribution as <code>AW3115</code>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    What is recorded always folds into the plan's fingerprint. It reaches the
                    <code>@Woven</code> annotation only when the weaver's detail is above
                    <code>NONE</code>. <a href="determinism.topic">Determinism</a> covers why a
                    changed value changes what counts as already woven.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Events">
                <p>
                    <code>observeApply()</code> asks to be told about each <code>ClassWoven</code>
                    event, published once a class is rewritten, verified and stamped. A
                    class the extension pass alone touched, or one the verifier refused, produces
                    none.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <code>PluginsLoaded</code> and <code>Prepared</code> reach every loaded plugin
                    regardless of <code>observeApply()</code>. <code>WeavingFinished</code>
                    arrives only if the program that built the weaver calls <code>finish()</code>
                    itself, which no shipped driver does. An observer cannot change what it is
                    told, and the bytes are never offered to it.
                    <a href="plugin-lifecycle.topic">The plugin lifecycle</a> has the four events
                    in order.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <note>
            <p>
                The annotation processor validates <code>@At</code> against the built-in points
                only when the target's class file can already be read. Otherwise it reports
                <code>AW1200</code> and defers the check to weave time. Where it does check,
                naming a contributed identifier is <code>AW1101</code> at compile time, whether or
                not your plugin is on the classpath. Name a built-in point, or keep that weave out
                of a module the processor compiles.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Two calls nothing reads back" id="inert">
        <p>
            Both are accepted, and both land on the registry the weaver was built with. Neither
            list is read again.
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>A <code>SelectorResolver</code> changes no match. What a selector does match is
                in <a href="selectors.topic">Selectors</a>, and its syntax in
                <a href="selector-grammar.topic">the selector grammar</a>.</p></li>
            <li><p>A <code>DiagnosticListener</code> is fed nothing. The weaver reports through
                the listener its driver installed, so keep your own record as you report —
                <a href="reporting.topic">Report rather than throw</a>.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What a plugin does not decide" id="limits">
        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Not yours to register</td><td>Where it comes from</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A policy refusing a class</td><td>Whoever builds the weaver sets it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Which weaves exist</td><td>A source the driver constructs itself</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The bytes handed back</td><td>Injectors write them, observers hear of them</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The built-in namespace</td><td>Its owner is the engine's own plugin</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            <a href="policy-and-safety.topic">Weave policy</a> has the first row and
            <a href="weave-discovery.topic">How a weave is found</a> the second.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Only a weaver you build loads one" id="reach">
        <warning>
            <p>
                No shipped driver loads a plugin of yours. The Maven goal, the agent, the weaving
                class loader and the testkit each assemble their weaver without
                <code>plugin(...)</code> and without <code>discoverPlugins(...)</code>, so the
                engine's own built-in plugin is the only one they load.
            </p>
        </warning>

        <p>
            Both calls belong to the program that builds the weaver.
            <code>Weaver.builder().plugin(...)</code> installs an instance you hand it, and
            <code>discoverPlugins(loader)</code> asks for a <code>ServiceLoader</code> lookup on
            that loader. Discovery happens only when a loader was set.
        </p>

        <p>
            Such a weaver gives every plugin an empty configuration, so your defaults are all you
            get. <a href="loading-plugins.topic">Loading and isolating plugins</a> covers discovery and the
            allowlist, and <a href="write-a-plugin.topic">Write a plugin</a> is the class itself.
        </p>
    </chapter>

